A new and altogether different screen excitement!

2
00:07:47,142 --> 00:07:53,142
Psycho is a horror, thriller, mystery film directed by Alfred Hitchcock.

3
00:11:40,714 --> 00:11:46,714
When larcenous real estate clerk Marion Crane goes on the lam with a wad of cash and hopes of starting a new life,
she ends up at the notorious Bates Motel, where manager Norman Bates cares for his housebound mother.

4
00:15:34,285 --> 00:15:40,285
Norman Bates, played by Anthony Perkins.

5
00:19:27,857 --> 00:19:33,857
Marion Crane, played by Janet Leigh.

6
00:23:21,428 --> 00:23:27,428
Lila Crane, played by Vera Miles.

7
00:27:15,000 --> 00:27:21,000
Sam Loomis, played by John Gavin.

8
00:31:08,571 --> 00:31:14,571
Milton Arbogast, played by Martin Balsam.

9
00:35:02,142 --> 00:35:08,142
Al Chambers, played by John McIntire.

10
00:38:55,714 --> 00:39:01,714
Psycho is a 1960 American horror film produced and directed by Alfred Hitchcock.

11
00:42:49,285 --> 00:42:55,285
The screenplay, written by Joseph Stefano, was based on the
1959 novel of the same name by Robert Bloch.

12
00:46:42,857 --> 00:46:48,857
The film stars Anthony Perkins, Janet Leigh, Vera
Miles, John Gavin and Martin Balsam.

13
00:50:36,428 --> 00:50:42,428
The plot centers on an encounter between on-the-run embezzler Marion Crane (Leigh),
shy motel proprietor Norman Bates (Perkins) and his disturbed mother.

14
00:54:30,000 --> 00:54:36,000
A private investigator (Balsam), Marion's lover Sam Loomis (Gavin) and
her sister Lila (Miles) investigate her disappearance.

15
00:58:23,571 --> 00:58:29,571
Psycho was seen as a departure from Hitchcock's previous film, North by Northwest (1959), as it was filmed on a
small budget in black-and-white by the crew of his television series Alfred Hitchcock Presents.

16
01:02:17,142 --> 01:02:23,142
Initially, the film divided critics due to its controversial subject matter, but audience
interest and outstanding box-office returns prompted a major critical re-evaluation.

17
01:06:10,714 --> 01:06:16,714
Psycho was a massive commercial success; from a budget of $806,947 ($6.2 million with 2025 inflation), the film gained $50
million ($384.5 million with 2025 inflation) at the box office, worldwide, not including the money made with rentals.

18
01:10:04,285 --> 01:10:10,285
Psycho was nominated for four Academy Awards, including Best Director
for Hitchcock and Best Supporting Actress for Leigh.

19
01:13:57,857 --> 01:14:03,857
Psycho is now considered one of Hitchcock's best films, and is
arguably his most famous and influential work.

20
01:17:51,428 --> 01:17:57,428
It has been hailed as a major work of cinematic art by international film critics and scholars who praise its
slick direction, tense atmosphere, impressive camerawork, memorable score and iconic performances.

21
01:21:45,000 --> 01:21:51,000
Additionally, the shower scene has become one of
the best-known in all of cinema.

22
01:25:38,571 --> 01:25:44,571
It is regarded as "the most heavily analyzed film in the long career of the most investigated director
in the history of American film" and often ranked among the greatest films of all time.

23
01:29:32,142 --> 01:29:38,142
It set a new level of acceptability for violence, deviant behavior and sexuality in American films,
and has been considered to be one of the earliest examples of the slasher film genre.

24
01:33:25,714 --> 01:33:31,714
After Hitchcock's death in 1980, Universal Pictures produced follow-ups: three
sequels, a remake, a made-for-television spin-off and a television series.

25
01:37:19,285 --> 01:37:25,285
In 1992, the Library of Congress deemed the film "culturally, historically, or aesthetically
significant" and selected it for preservation in the United States National Film Registry.

26
01:41:12,857 --> 01:41:18,857
The film explores themes of hotel, clerk, detective, shower, arizona.

27
01:45:06,428 --> 01:45:12,428
The tone is dark and mind_bending and thought_provoking.
